<h1>transitive reduction</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
The transitive reduction of a <a href="directedGraph.html"><em>directed graph</em></a> G is the directed graph G' with the smallest number of <a href="edge.html"><em>edges</em></a> such that for every <a href="path.html"><em>path</em></a> between <a href="vertex.html"><em>vertices</em></a> in G, G' has a path between those vertices.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="reducedDigraph.html"><em>reduced digraph</em></a>, <a href="transitiveClosure.html"><em>transitive closure</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Informally G' is the minimal graph with the same connectivity as G. After abstract of  <strong>A. V. Aho, M. R. Garey</strong>, and <strong>J. D. Ullman</strong>. <em>The transitive reduction of a directed graph</em>. SIAM Journal on Computing, 1:131--137, 1972.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>Implementation</h2>
Steven Skiena's Algorist/Stony Brook summary and links to <a href="https://algorist.com/problems/Transitive_Closure_and_Reduction.html" target="_blank">implementations (C++, Java, and Mathematica)</a>.

